`karr move 3 in-progress` refuses without a claim ("Status 'in-progress' requires a claim"), because the board's `require_claim` statuses are enforced in Role::TaskMutation/apply_status_change. `karr create "x" --status in-progress` takes the same status and creates a card in progress that nobody holds: Cmd::Create never consults `status_requires_claim`. kanban-md's create takes `--claim` (doc: "claim task for an agent (use 'agent-name' to generate)"); karr's does not, so the only correct sequence today is create, then move with --claim: two round trips for one intent.

## Repro

    karr create x --status in-progress                        # Created task 2: x  (unclaimed, in progress)
    karr create y --status todo && karr move 3 in-progress    # refused, needs --claim

## What to build

- `create --claim NAME`: stamps claimed_by/claimed_at exactly as `move --claim` does (same helper, not a copy) and writes the activity-log entry under that name.
- `create --status S` where S requires a claim and no `--claim` was given is refused with the k263-shaped message, last line the working command: `karr create x --status in-progress --claim NAME`.
- The default status (backlog) is unaffected; a board with `require_claim` empty behaves as today.

Tests in t/, Changes entry.

Pointers: Cmd::Create validates status, priority, class and due around line 200 (validate_status and friends); the require_claim check belongs right there, through $store->status_requires_claim, the same method Role::TaskMutation/apply_status_change consults. Claim stamping is whatever move --claim does (claimed_by and claimed_at through Role::ClaimTimeout); reuse it, do not copy it. The activity-log entry for create carries only the git identity today; with --claim its agent field is the claim name, as move's entry is.

